.TH OD 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
od \- dump files in octal and other formats
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B od
.RB [ \-A
.IR base ]
.RB [ \-j
.IR skip ]
.RB [ \-N
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B od
writes an offset\-and\-formatted\-data dump of its input to standard
output, 16 bytes per output row (a short final row is written with
fewer). By default, offsets are octal and each row's data is 8
octal\-formatted 2\-byte units. Multiple
.I file
operands are read as one logical concatenated stream, the same way
.BR cat (1)
concatenates them.
.PP
Without
.BR \-v ,
any run of output rows that would be byte\-for\-byte identical to the
row immediately before it (ignoring the differing offset) is replaced
by a single line containing only an asterisk. Only a full 16\-byte
row can ever be elided this way; a shorter final row is always
printed.
.PP
A trailing line giving the final total offset, with no data, is
always printed at the end (unless
.B \-A n
is given) \(em this is how a reader can see the exact total byte
count even when it lands exactly on a row boundary, or was elided
entirely.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-A " base"
Select the base that byte offsets are printed in.
.I base
must be exactly one of:
.RS
.TP
.B d
Decimal.
.TP
.B o
Octal (the default when
.B \-A
is not given).
.TP
.B x
Hexadecimal.
.TP
.B n
No offsets are printed at all, on any output line (including the
final byte\-count line).
.RE
.TP
.BI \-j " skip"
Skip
.I skip
bytes of input before dumping. Skipping past the end of the input is
not an error. See OPERANDS below for
.IR skip 's
own numeric syntax.
.TP
.BI \-N " count"
Dump at most
.I count
bytes of input. See OPERANDS below for
.IR count 's
own numeric syntax.
.TP
.BI \-t " type"
Select the output format for each unit of data.
.I type
is one of:
.RS
.TP
.BI x N
Unsigned hexadecimal, in units of
.I N
bytes.
.TP
.BI o N
Unsigned octal, in units of
.I N
bytes.
.TP
.BI d N
Signed decimal, in units of
.I N
bytes.
.TP
.BI u N
Unsigned decimal, in units of
.I N
bytes.
.TP
.B c
Character dump (see below). Takes no size suffix.
.RE
.RS
.I N
must be exactly 1, 2, 4, or 8 for the
.BR x / o / d / u
forms; the alternative letter\-coded sizes
.RB ( C , " S" , " I" , " L" )
that POSIX also allows are not accepted. When
.B \-t
is not given at all, the default is as if
.B "\-t o2"
had been given. Multi\-byte units are decoded in this platform's own
native (little\-endian) byte order.
.RE
.TP
.B \-v
Disable the repeated\-row elision described in DESCRIPTION: every row
is printed in full, even when identical to the one before it.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I skip ", " count
A numeric argument to
.B \-j
or
.BR \-N ,
given as a decimal number, a hexadecimal number with a leading
.BR 0x / 0X ,
or an octal number with a leading zero, optionally followed by a
single multiplier suffix:
.B b
(\(mu512),
.B k
(\(mu1024), or
.B m
(\(mu1048576).
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If no
.I file
operand is given at all, standard input is used.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
The character dump
.RB ( "\-t c" )
writes
.BR \e\e ", " \ea ", " \eb ", " \ef ", " \en ", " \er ", " \et ", "
and
.B \ev
for the corresponding control characters, a NUL byte as
.BR \e0 ,
every other printable byte as itself, and every other byte as a
three\-digit octal escape.
.PP
The named\-character format
.RB ( "\-t a" ),
the floating\-point format
.RB ( "\-t f" ),
and the letter\-coded unit sizes
.RB ( C / S / I / L )
that POSIX
.B od
also defines are not implemented; requesting any of them is a usage
error rather than a silent misinterpretation.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input was dumped successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred: an invalid option or option argument, an
unsupported
.B \-t
type, or a file that could not be opened.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR wc (1)
